  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A cold front arriving today will trigger \nscattered showers and a few thunderstorms, along with breezy \nwinds through Saturday morning. \n\nStarting the morning off with low clouds across the Piedmont, \nand areas of locally dense fog across portions of the New River\nValley where skies are mostly clear. These conditions will \nimprove quickly after 10 AM as wind speeds gradually increase \nahead of an approaching cold front. \n\nToday will provide the last of the summer-like temperatures for\na while as warm air pools ahead of the approaching front. With \nhighs reaching into the mid/upper 70s across the mountains, and \ninto the 80s further east, will be testing the record high \ntemperature of 85 for Roanoke if clouds can break up early \nenough. Afternoon highs will reach within a few degrees of \nrecords for several other locations as well.\n\nThe cold front will enter southeast West Virginia from the \nnorthwest during late afternoon/early evening, triggering a \nbroken line of showers and possibly a thunderstorm or two along \nits leading edge. The front will pass east quickly, reaching \nthe Highway 29 corridor between Lynchburg and Danville by around\n8 PM and exiting our area to the east by 10 PM. Forecasting \nmainly shower activity this evening across the mountains due to \nlimited energy, but cannot rule out a few storms developing \nacross the Piedmont given the unusually warm temperatures there.\nAny potential for damaging wind gusts appears to be highly\nlimited. Showers will persist across our area through much of \nthe night as a supporting upper level disturbance passes across \nthe central Appalachians, with lingering rain forecast to \ntapper off around sunrise Saturday.\n\nGiven the strength of this cold front, wind gusts will increase\nto between 15 mph and locally 25 mph from the west-southwest \nduring mid-morning and through mid-afternoon, and will then\nincrease into the 20 mph to locally 35 mph range from the \nnorthwest in the wake of the front's passage. Winds will remain \nbreezy overnight, and through much of Saturday before gradually \ndiminishing after sunset Saturday evening.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Confidence is high for frost and pockets of \nfreezing temperatures to occur across the mountains on Sunday \nmorning.\n\nWith wind speeds decreasing and skies clearing Saturday night, \nconfidence is high for areas of frost to develop across the \nmountains and possibly even the foothills east of the Blue Ridge.\nThere are also signals that some of the more sheltered mountain\nvalleys will fall below freezing by sunrise Sunday, effectively\nending the growing season there. Confidence is not high that \nthe coverage of sub-freezing temperatures will extend beyond \nthose locations however, given the possibility that winds may \nremain just breezy enough to mix the slightly warmer air aloft \ndown to ground-level. Still, day shift may opt to issue a \nFreeze Watch for some mountain locations later today if newer \ndata supports it.\n\nAfter another chilly night on Monday night, temperatures will \nmoderate to near normal for mid-October through the remainder of\nthe next workweek. No hazardous weather is expected during this\nperiod.",
